<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>1.2.2: Modeling with Equations to Find Edges in Platonic Solids</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00071</md:content-id>
    <md:title>1.2.2: Modeling with Equations to Find Edges in Platonic Solids</md:title>
    <md:uuid>72c98e43-8efb-4c6b-bab6-532d658a0bbc</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity
</title>
<para>These three figures are called Platonic solids.</para>
<div class="os-raise-d-flex os-raise-justify-content-evenly">
  <para>Tetrahedron <figure><media alt="A tetrahedron."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/932b14f930cd2a440ea2f4073f554b2661cd2016"/></media></figure></para>
  <para>Cube<figure><media alt="A cube."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/cd65700f380e1eba6a9b1867e41604878abd7f53"/></media></figure></para>
  <para>Dodecahedron<figure><media alt="A dodecahderon. A 3-D shape with 12 pentagonal faces."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/d3a80d936b95871fc28291180c7ae8ef53e7d932"/></media></figure></para>
</div>
<table class="os-raise-doubleheadertable">
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis"/>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">faces</entry>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">vertices</entry>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">edges</entry>
    </row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry scope="row" class="emphasis">tetrahedron</entry>
      <entry>4</entry>
      <entry>4</entry>
      <entry>6</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry scope="row" class="emphasis">cube</entry>
      <entry/>
      <entry/>
      <entry/>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry scope="row" class="emphasis">dodecahedron</entry>
      <entry>12</entry>
      <entry>20</entry>
      <entry>30</entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>
<para>The table shows the number of faces, vertices, and edges for the tetrahedron and dodecahedron.</para>
<para>Determine the following values regarding the cube.</para>
<div class="os-raise-ib-pset" data-button-text="Check" data-content-id="889d6eea-e625-4061-bbf4-ef3141914678" data-retry-limit="0" data-schema-version="1.0">
  <exercise data-content-id="a4de0230-e91f-48b4-bbfc-e1250afb4a9b" data-problem-comparator="integer" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="6">
    <problem>
      <para> Number of faces. </para>
      <para> Enter your answer here: </para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para> Correct! 6.</para>
    </solution>
        </exercise>
  <exercise data-content-id="cdf77a10-ae30-4d5e-b741-0050e643db28" data-problem-comparator="integer" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="8">
    <problem>
      <para> Number of edges. </para>
      <para> Enter your answer here: </para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para> Correct! 8.</para>
    </solution>
        </exercise>
  <exercise data-content-id="13797257-27a3-4f32-adc3-da4166352174" data-problem-comparator="integer" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="12">
    <problem>
      <para> Number of vertices. </para>
      <para> Enter your answer here: </para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para> Correct! 12.</para>
    </solution>
        </exercise>
  <!--Do not edit below this line-->
  <solution> </solution>
  </div>
<para>There are some interesting relationships between the number of faces (\( F \)), edges (\( E \)), and vertices (\( V
  \)) in all Platonic solids. </para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item>What do you notice about the relationships between the numbers of faces, edges, and vertices of the Platonic
    solids?</item>
</list>
<exercise>
  <problem>
  </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para> Your answer may vary, but here are some examples.</para>
    <list>
      <item>I noticed all the numbers are even.</item>
      <item>I noticed the number of faces is less than or equal to the number of vertices, or \(F \leq V\)</item>
      <item>I noticed the number of edges is always greater than the number of faces, or \(E \gt F\).</item>
    </list>
  </solution></exercise>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="5" type="enumerated">
  <item>What do you wonder about the relationships between the numbers of faces, edges, and vertices of the Platonic
    solids? </item>
</list>
<exercise>
  <problem>
  </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para> Your answer may vary, but here are some examples.</para>
    <list>
      <item>I wonder if the number of edges is always less than the sum of the number of faces and the number of vertices,
        or \(E \lt F + V\). </item>
      <item>I wonder if there is an equation that connects all of the variables.</item>
    </list>
  </solution></exercise>
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> There is a relationship that can be expressed with an equation using all three values: \(F, V, E\). Write the
        equation that represents how the three values are related.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>The number of edges is 2 less than the sum of the other two numbers, or \(V + F - 2 = E\).</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->
